  "body": "Research only - no code from this issue. Pick never takes it for code (AGENTS.md \"needs-research\").\n\n**Why.** Conversational systems (shop-floor forms on the control or a PC: e.g. Kentech's KipwareM/T, Mazatrol, PathPilot conversational, Heidenhain Klartext cycles, LinuxCNC NGCGUI) generate G-code from a few numbers with no CAD model: bolt-hole circles, frames and pockets, keyways, grooves, threads, facing to size. Users who never open CAD (e.g. a WorkBee owner on Discord: making a 3D part from a 2D sketch is \"too much of a hassle\") would use CamCraft for these if it offered them. Asked by the user (mkinney) 2026-11-20.\n\n**Clean-room boundary.** Other vendors' public feature lists are used only as a checklist of *which* operations shops expect. Never copied: their wording, UI labels, parameter names, screen layouts or dialog flows. Each operation's behaviour is specced from machining handbooks and public G-code standards, as for any operation. Whether AGENTS.md's reference list should name \"public feature lists of other products, for gap finding only\" is a question for the maintainer.\n\n**Open questions**\n1. Which conversational operations are common across systems (mill and lathe)? A table: operation, which systems have it, what inputs it needs in machining terms.\n2. Which of those CamCraft already covers (existing operations that would work from a sketch or stock alone), which need only a new \"from numbers\" front end, and which are genuinely new toolpath work?\n3. Where does it live: a \"quick op\" that makes its own sketch geometry and a normal operation (undoable commands + MCP tools), versus a separate wizard? Recommendation needed.\n4. Output style: canned cycles and cutter comp where the post supports them (conversational users expect short, readable programs) versus expanded moves.\n5. Priority order for sub-issues, by how many users it helps.\n\n**Done means:** the questions answered in comments here, the sub-issues filed (each with one P*, one effort:*, area:*, source:agent, a \"Done means\" line and \"Part of #this\"), linked back, `needs-research` dropped and this umbrella closed.\n\nRelated: #2227 (Heidenhain Klartext dialect), #1880 (SVG import).\n",
